{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1532 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f94fea71933e53fa255f49e8ef22fd0b8b757083fbb8903df3f387a9456fef31" :edb/db-before-t 1531 :edb/db-after-t 1532 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534844 50 #inst "2026-10-08T08:48:41.398Z" 13194139534844 true] [17592186063958 1100 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x) = x^2$ on the interval $[0,2].$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186063958 1100 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x) = x^2$ on the interval $[0,2]$." 13194139534844 false] [17592186063958 1145 "The function $f(x)$  is a polynomial, so it is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$ \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c \\in (0,2)$ such that \n\n$$\nf'(c)   = \\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(0)}{2-(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf'(c) = 2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =2 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186063958 1145 "The function $f(x)$ is a polynomial, so it is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$.\n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c \\in (0,2)$ such that\n\n$$\nf'(c) = \\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(c) &= (f(2) - f(0))/(2 - (0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{2} - 0^{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) = 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf'(c) = 2c\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2c &= 2 \\\\\nc &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534844 false] [17592186063959 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ below is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4)$. Complete the sentence below.\n\n> *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ \\,$.*\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/288/Source/Images/50f7d14f01067a1e593877f08d34b5b2.png)" 13194139534844 false] [17592186063959 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ below is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ \\, .$ *\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/5672cc68e55078623f34f01650d3e82bd3cee62e6ca97fabcd2287f2a865501a.png)" 13194139534844 true] [17592186063959 1145 "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n> *There exists a point $c \\in (a,b)$ such that\n> $f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}$.*\n\nIn our case, we have $a = -1$ and $b=4$, so the above statement becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(c) &= (f(4) - f(-1))/(4 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6 - 2}{4 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n> *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that* $f'(c) =\\dfrac {4}{5}$." 13194139534844 false] [17592186063959 1145 "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b),$ the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c \\in (a,b)$ such that\n$f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.$*\n\nIn our case, we have $a = -1$ and $b=4,$ so the above statement becomes \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-1)}{4-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2}{4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that* $f'(c) =\\dfrac {4}{5}.$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186063960 1100 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/288/Source/Images/412e4f68cffe765264fae545418ef7fe.png)" 13194139534844 false] [17592186063960 1100 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[−2,2]?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/e680c2e80874a4c2e8b4d31fcc7a6ba42a3c55fb7c6f559e42f3cd785b22e3ed.png)" 13194139534844 true] [17592186063960 1145 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]$, the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$.\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{I}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the sharp corners at $x=\\pm 2$ are not in $(-2,2)$.\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{II}$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2$. So it is not continuous on $[-2,2]$, and therefore it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{III}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that although the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2$, it is right-continuous at $x=-2$.\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{IV}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=0$. So, it is not differentiable at $x=0$, which means it is not differentiable on $(-2,2)$. Therefore, it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nIn conclusion, only diagrams $\\textrm{I}$ and $\\textrm{III}$ show functions that satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]$." 13194139534844 false] [17592186063960 1145 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{I}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the sharp corners at $x=\\pm 2$ are not in $(-2,2).$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{II}$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2.$ So it is not continuous on $[-2,2],$ and therefore it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{III}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that although the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2,$ it is right-continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{IV}$ is continuous on $[−2,2]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=0.$ So, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(−2,2).$  Therefore, it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nIn conclusion, only diagrams $\\textrm{I}$ and $\\textrm{III}$ show functions that satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186064036 1100 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]$?\n\n1. $f(x)= 3^{x} - 1$\n2. $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$\n3. $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$" 13194139534844 false] [17592186064036 1100 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)= 3^{x} - 1$\n\n- $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$\n\n- $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186064036 1145 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]$, the function must be continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$.\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 3^{x} - 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty)$. In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$. Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n- The function $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty)$. In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n- The function $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x = 0$, hence, it is not continuous at\n$x = 0$, and so it is not continuous on $[0,1]$. Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]$." 13194139534844 false] [17592186064036 1145 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 3^{x} - 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x = 0,$ hence, it is not continuous at \n$x = 0,$ and so it is not continuous on $[0,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1].$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186064075 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314179 13194139534844 true] [17592186064075 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314186 13194139534844 true] [17592186064075 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314193 13194139534844 true] [17592186064076 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314137 13194139534844 true] [17592186064076 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314144 13194139534844 true] [17592186064076 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314217 13194139534844 true] [17592186064076 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314224 13194139534844 true] [17592186064076 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314231 13194139534844 true] [17592186064077 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314158 13194139534844 true] [17592186064077 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314196 13194139534844 true] [17592186064077 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314203 13194139534844 true] [17592186064077 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314238 13194139534844 true] [17592186064077 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314245 13194139534844 true] [17592186064192 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314151 13194139534844 true] [17592186064192 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314165 13194139534844 true] [17592186064192 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314172 13194139534844 true] [17592186064192 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314210 13194139534844 true] [17592186064540 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534844 true] [17592186064540 1100 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for\n\n$f(x) = x^{2}$ on the interval $[- 8, 6]$." 13194139534844 false] [17592186064540 1100 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for \n $f(x)={x}^{2}$ on the interval $[-8,6].$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186064540 1145 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-8,6]$ and differentiable on $(-8,6).$ \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (-8,6)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=-8$ and $b=6$ into the above gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(6)-f(-8)}{6-(-8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{2}-(-8)^{2}}{14} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =-2 \\\\ c & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186064541 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534844 true] [17592186064541 1100 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for\n\n$f(x) =-3\\sqrt{x} + 2$ on the interval $[0, 2]$." 13194139534844 false] [17592186064541 1100 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for \n $f(x)=-3\\sqrt{x}+2$ on the interval $[0,2].$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186064541 1145 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (0,2)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(0)}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3\\sqrt{2}+2)-(-3\\sqrt{0}+2)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\sqrt{x}+2)=-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{c}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{c}} & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{c}} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{c} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ c & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186064736 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534844 true] [17592186064736 1100 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[- 1, 1]$?\n\n1. $g(x) = \\mid x \\mid$\n2. $s(x) = \\frac{1}{x}$\n3. $h(x) = 2x$" 13194139534844 false] [17592186064736 1100 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $g(x)=|x|$\n- $s(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $h(x)=2x$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186064736 1145 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ but has a corner at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not continuous at $x=0$, which means it is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function III satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314137 1017 #edb/ref 17592186314138 13194139534844 true] [17592186314137 1089 "q-125237" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314137 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534844 true] [17592186314137 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/74850221ee168c6911ee5b6c319d2128f67149f81790528940822e253905fc0f.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-5,3]$ and differentiable on  $(-5,3).$  What is the missing part of the sentence in the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314137 1113 #uuid "70a9e682-5225-5573-9ac6-75c97731ce3c" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314137 1145 "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-5$ and $b=3.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-5)}{3-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-2}{3+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$*" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314138 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314139 13194139534844 true] [17592186314138 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314140 13194139534844 true] [17592186314138 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314141 13194139534844 true] [17592186314138 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314142 13194139534844 true] [17592186314138 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314143 13194139534844 true] [17592186314138 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534844 true] [17592186314138 1041 "selection" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314138 1104 #edb/ref 17592186314141 13194139534844 true] [17592186314138 1110 #uuid "121e061c-8f59-5d38-a696-c7283ddd23ef" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314139 1034 "{f}^{′}(c)=-2" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314139 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314139 1111 #uuid "df0c4859-5dea-5999-8d14-38364fcd450d" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314140 1034 "{f}^{′}(c)=0" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314140 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314140 1111 #uuid "a062fd1a-a751-5522-9439-7c2d18fd2251" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314141 1034 "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314141 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314141 1111 #uuid "24c7d6cd-76f1-5136-b989-3e280d92b241" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314142 1034 "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314142 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314142 1111 #uuid "75e29608-d101-5e74-a0f9-aeb93dacd868" 13194139534844 true