 [17592186314172 1017 #edb/ref 17592186314173 13194139534844 true] [17592186314172 1089 "q-127014" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314172 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534844 true] [17592186314172 1100 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3]?$\n\n- $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$\n- $r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x-1|)$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314172 1113 #uuid "937932cc-d4b5-5d08-ac33-37cd1b49f559" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314172 1145 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $r(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function I satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3].$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314174 13194139534844 true] [17592186314173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314175 13194139534844 true] [17592186314173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314176 13194139534844 true] [17592186314173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314177 13194139534844 true] [17592186314173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314178 13194139534844 true] [17592186314173 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534844 true] [17592186314173 1041 "selection" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314173 1104 #edb/ref 17592186314175 13194139534844 true] [17592186314173 1110 #uuid "5a5bd924-cec8-57d3-bd28-99edc3da23a3" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314174 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534844 true] [17592186314174 1111 #uuid "dd460f4e-4fe7-5931-8275-17aa12236be4" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314175 1034 "I only" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314175 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534844 true] [17592186314175 1111 #uuid "cac70e6a-f7ad-50a8-8322-28dc617ec95e" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314176 1034 "III only" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314176 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534844 true] [17592186314176 1111 #uuid "ab198bc9-1f18-5552-b9d9-d52ef9123f99" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314177 1034 "None" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534844 true] [17592186314177 1111 #uuid "d9bdeeec-7878-57c0-a3d3-290aab7782bb" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314178 1034 "II only" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534844 true] [17592186314178 1111 #uuid "e55c8538-8410-539f-8770-7a2682e86508" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314179 1017 #edb/ref 17592186314180 13194139534844 true] [17592186314179 1089 "q-2305" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314179 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534844 true] [17592186314179 1100 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+x-2$ on the interval $[-1,2].$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314179 1113 #uuid "5f9d35ca-8f34-57a2-b6aa-de34e247b461" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314179 1145 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,2]$ and differentiable on $(-1,2),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (-1,2)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=-1$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(-1)}{2-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\cdot {2}^{2}+2-2)-(3\\cdot (-1)^{2}+(-1)-2)}{2+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-0}{3} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}+x-2)=6x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=6c+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6c+1 & =4 \\\\ 6c & =3 \\\\ c & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314181 13194139534844 true] [17592186314180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314182 13194139534844 true] [17592186314180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314183 13194139534844 true] [17592186314180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314184 13194139534844 true] [17592186314180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314185 13194139534844 true] [17592186314180 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534844 true] [17592186314180 1041 "selection" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314180 1104 #edb/ref 17592186314181 13194139534844 true] [17592186314180 1110 #uuid "8be1f33a-6b9a-5b58-972e-22a94646a1fa" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314181 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314181 1111 #uuid "77e98f3b-efce-556b-9825-d61cd1f29838" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314182 1034 "2" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314182 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314182 1111 #uuid "0a94857f-1624-5820-94b9-6f525a7eaac8" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314183 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314183 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314183 1111 #uuid "a6245ad7-2d47-5544-aada-ffffa59b4325" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314184 1034 "6" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314184 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314184 1111 #uuid "ca4689be-f51c-534e-9612-8361bb585b22" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314185 1034 "\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314185 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314185 1111 #uuid "67427346-3ea0-5b13-b931-b7414820c29c" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314186 1017 #edb/ref 17592186314187 13194139534844 true] [17592186314186 1089 "q-2342" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314186 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534844 true] [17592186314186 1100 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for \n $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x+5$ on the interval $[1,4].$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314186 1113 #uuid "3da87fee-0786-5e85-9e1d-628e337cb883" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314186 1145 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[1,4]$ and differentiable on $(1,4),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (1,4)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=1$ and $b=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{({4}^{2}-2\\cdot 4+5)-({1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1+5)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-4}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-2x+5)=2x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=2c-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c-2 & =3 \\\\ 2c & =5 \\\\ c & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314188 13194139534844 true] [17592186314187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314189 13194139534844 true] [17592186314187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314190 13194139534844 true] [17592186314187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314191 13194139534844 true] [17592186314187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314192 13194139534844 true] [17592186314187 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534844 true] [17592186314187 1041 "selection" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314187 1104 #edb/ref 17592186314190 13194139534844 true] [17592186314187 1110 #uuid "7e68ded2-3d31-5158-9654-23c0cf579fae" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314188 1034 "-\\frac{5}{2}\\,,5" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314188 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314188 1111 #uuid "fbb75918-34d8-5024-82b4-b9392f2119fc" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314189 1034 "\\frac{3}{5}\\," 13194139534844 true] [17592186314189 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314189 1111 #uuid "8c88a1ac-4c20-508f-aab6-31c06f787e03" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314190 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314190 1111 #uuid "dc332d90-894b-531a-9c8f-2fb91cf04a05" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314191 1034 "\\pm 5" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314191 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314191 1111 #uuid "591d829f-6048-5881-bff8-c4aef2776230" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314192 1034 "-\\frac{5}{2}\\," 13194139534844 true] [17592186314192 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314192 1111 #uuid "1cda0e1a-40fc-5872-bd05-97da86b8a904" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314193 1017 #edb/ref 17592186314194 13194139534844 true] [17592186314193 1089 "q-345063" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314193 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534844 true] [17592186314193 1100 "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x)=5\\sqrt{x}-2$ on the interval $[4,9].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314193 1113 #uuid "ba9aff4f-f2e6-5d6a-bbda-2b487e4d8919" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314193 1145 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[4,9]$ and differentiable on $(4,9),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (4,9)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=4$ and $b=9$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(9)-f(4)}{9-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5\\sqrt{9}-2)-(5\\sqrt{4}-2)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-8}{5} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\sqrt{x}-2)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{c}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{c}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{c} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ c & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314195 13194139534844 true] [17592186314194 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534844 true] [17592186314194 1041 "field-1" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314194 1104 #edb/ref 17592186314195 13194139534844 true] [17592186314194 1110 #uuid "4a11827e-ce9d-5354-a735-fdc0d1a904b7" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314195 1034 "\\frac{25}{4}" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314195 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534844 true] [17592186314195 1111 #uuid "c5416af2-c5a6-5183-8a75-14862a9fb852" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314196 1017 #edb/ref 17592186314197 13194139534844 true] [17592186314196 1089 "q-48396" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314196 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534844 true] [17592186314196 1100 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,4]?$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314196 1113 #uuid "8670d161-54b2-5d76-9f17-77b18f090a9c" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314196 1145 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function  $g(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$   Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(1,4).$  Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$ and is not defined for $x\\in (-\\infty ,1].$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[-1,4].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Hence, $h(x)$  is not continuous on $[-1,4].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $f(x)$  is continuous on $[-1,4]$ but has a cusp at $x=2.$  Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem." 13194139534844 true] [17592186314197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314198 13194139534844 true] [17592186314197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314199 13194139534844 true] [17592186314197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314200 13194139534844 true] [17592186314197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314201 13194139534844 true] [17592186314197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314202 13194139534844 true] [17592186314197 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534844 true] [17592186314197 1041 "selection" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314197 1104 #edb/ref 17592186314201 13194139534844 true] [17592186314197 1110 #uuid "0f453603-c815-5323-a538-420797e8c5c6" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314198 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/b629cd9686bb3f9b671a9412552ccd4161cb832d8e8426ee83a8955b81cc5831.png" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314198 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534844 true] [17592186314198 1111 #uuid "7b969cfd-7cc1-55a7-9880-7b61b434f5b8" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314199 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/6cd605b5533bf0211bf75d3cc1d461fe41ee382e8799bd2337c4a53627eb0ca9.png" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314199 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534844 true] [17592186314199 1111 #uuid "4a02ae06-70c4-57f4-a3e2-1626b85c36e4" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314200 1034 "/