 it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,4).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{4}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ is not defined on $(-\\infty ,1).$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,4].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function II satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4].$" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314212 13194139534844 true] [17592186314211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314213 13194139534844 true] [17592186314211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314214 13194139534844 true] [17592186314211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314215 13194139534844 true] [17592186314211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314216 13194139534844 true] [17592186314211 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534844 true] [17592186314211 1041 "selection" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314211 1104 #edb/ref 17592186314216 13194139534844 true] [17592186314211 1110 #uuid "d7271472-91ee-5dab-9fa6-a3d4527d95f5" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314212 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314212 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534844 true] [17592186314212 1111 #uuid "5c246d93-1a7e-5ac0-bc7c-075112e3ff7c" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314213 1034 "I only" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314213 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534844 true] [17592186314213 1111 #uuid "ff342493-7ac1-5c81-9e90-11e24667c2dc" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314214 1034 "III only" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314214 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534844 true] [17592186314214 1111 #uuid "350d3a49-76f7-5443-8488-eac6e90e4e15" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314215 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314215 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534844 true] [17592186314215 1111 #uuid "99185650-3aca-5788-80ed-0299e529036e" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314216 1034 "II only" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314216 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534844 true] [17592186314216 1111 #uuid "db8e3319-4087-53ea-ab8e-545a388b1106" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314217 1017 #edb/ref 17592186314218 13194139534844 true] [17592186314217 1089 "q-73347" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314217 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534844 true] [17592186314217 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/ff919b53932bf52cdfb896ba528eccace648e4b840af1d1cd495299fe1b09119.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-4,2]$ and differentiable on $(-4,2).$ Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-4,2)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314217 1113 #uuid "8bd2fd41-851a-51b6-b403-fb1b83809bc9" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314217 1145 "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-4$ and $b=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(-4)}{2-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{2+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-4,2)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{2}.$*" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314219 13194139534844 true] [17592186314218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314220 13194139534844 true] [17592186314218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314221 13194139534844 true] [17592186314218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314222 13194139534844 true] [17592186314218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314223 1