 function must be continuous on $[-1,5]$ and differentiable on $(-1,5).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $p(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,5]$ and differentiable on $(-1,5)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,5].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)$  is continuous on $[-1,5]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=2.$  Hence, $g(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,5).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, $q(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,5].$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,5]$ but has corner points at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ Hence, $r(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$ and $x=4,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,5).$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem." 13194139534844 true] [17592186314239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314240 13194139534844 true] [17592186314239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314241 13194139534844 true] [17592186314239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314242 13194139534844 true] [17592186314239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314243 13194139534844 true] [17592186314239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314244 13194139534844 true] [17592186314239 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534844 true] [17592186314239 1041 "selection" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314239 1104 #edb/ref 17592186314244 13194139534844 true] [17592186314239 1110 #uuid "73e1446c-a3c5-5de3-95d0-838f6f5971ec" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314240 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/926d044961d7c54611f2693d33e245d20fb446d9b32527d134ce19f1dd39f408.p