139202fa4e" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314245 1145 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $h(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,3].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ but has a corner point at $x=2.$ Hence, $p(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Hence, $r(x)$  is not continuous on $[-1,3].$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$ Hence, $t(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,3).$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem." 13194139534844 true] [17592186314246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314247 13194139534844 true] [17592186314246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314248 13194139534844 true] [17592186314246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314249 13194139534844 true] [17592186314246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314250 13194139534844 true] [17592186314246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314251 13194139534844 true] [17592186314246 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534844 true] [17592186314246 1041 "selection" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314246 1104 #edb/ref 17592186314247 13194139534844 true] [17592186314246 1110 #uuid "b85ffea3-7ffa-59c3-baf6-91fdff82d3bb" 13194139534844 true] [17592186314247 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Acad